//@ exit: 101

// `TODO("why")` is an implementation that has not been written. It TYPE-CHECKS, and panics
// with its message if it is ever reached.
//
// Typed `never`, which is what lets it stand where a value is wanted: a whole function body,
// one arm of a match, an argument. The surrounding code checks as if the hole were any other
// expression, so a half-written function is still a function.
//
// It was a COMPILE error first, and the first real use argued it down. `std::fs`'s Windows
// half is a wall of `TODO`s, and erroring at the DEFINITION took the whole module out on
// Windows: a program calling only `fs::read` -- implemented everywhere, touching no hole --
// collected five errors about functions it never mentions. A marker meant to say "this part
// is missing" was saying "this library is unusable".
//
// The cost of the runtime form is real and worth naming: a `TODO` can ship, and nothing in a
// signature warns the caller. What would buy that back without the blast radius is an error
// at the CALL site rather than the definition -- `fs::read` compiles, `fs::mkdir(p)` does not
// -- which needs the checker to mark a holed function and diagnose its callers. TODO.md
// carries that as the design worth having.
//
// The line before the hole runs, which is the whole point: work proceeds around it.

use std::io;

fn classify(n: i64) -> str {
    if n > 0 {
        "positive"
    } else {
        TODO("the negative case")
    }
}

fn main() {
    io::println(classify(1));
    io::println(classify(-1));
}
